To find hidden links and secrets using ProjectDiscovery's Katana, LinkFinder, and SecretFinder, you need to pipe the crawled URLs from Katana directly into the Python tools.
Here is the quickest way to chain these tools together in your terminal.
## 1. Katana to LinkFinder
LinkFinder analyzes JavaScript files to find endpoints. You must configure Katana to output only JS files, then pass them to LinkFinder using xargs. [1] 

katana -u https://example.com -field file -extension js -silent | xargs -I % python3 LinkFinder.py -i % -o cli

## 2. Katana to SecretFinder
SecretFinder looks for sensitive data (API keys, tokens) inside JavaScript files. Use the same piping logic. [2, 3] 

katana -u https://example.com -field file -extension js -silent | xargs -I % python3 SecretFinder.py -i % -o cli

## 3. All-in-One Automation (Recommended)
If you want to run a single command that saves Katana's output to a text file and runs both tools automatically, use this script:

# 1. Crawl and save JS links
katana -u https://example.com -field file -extension js -silent -o js_links.txt
# 2. Run LinkFinder on all discovered linkswhile read -r url; do python3 LinkFinder.py -i "$url" -o cli; done < js_links.txt
# 3. Run SecretFinder on all discovered linkswhile read -r url; do python3 SecretFinder.py -i "$url" -o cli; done < js_links.txt

## Critical Setup Flags Used

* -field file: Tells Katana to output the raw file URL.
* -extension js: Filters the crawler to only output JavaScript files.
* -silent: Suppresses the banner so only clean URLs are piped.

